# Day 13 – Generative AI Learning Journey
**Date:** 28 July 2026

## What I did today
- Started High Reliability Review (HRR) Part I – Foundations as a formal standard/manual, not a prompt
- Clarified that AI (and humans) should not load 350 pages at once; use layered retrieval: Core Principles + relevant Module + current case
- Converted long framework content into clean printable PDF and Word formats without changing original text
- Designed a Class 10 chemistry session opening for Acids, Bases and Salts using pattern recognition and mystery framing

## What I learned
- Large frameworks should be reference libraries, not single prompts
- Reliability systems work by loading only the relevant layer for the current task
- Scientific concepts often start from observed patterns, not perfect definitions
- Presentation work is useful, but should not replace locking operational skills

## One small win
- Established the correct architecture for HRR: core principles always loaded, modules loaded only when needed

## Tomorrow’s focus
- Choose one operational skill (DPR v2 or F-347 or F568A) and lock it with a real case test
- Do not expand HRR into new chapters until at least one existing module is frozen
